COPYRIGHT AND OWNERSHIP — DECISION REQUIRED
===========================================

Ownership of this site's source code has not been set. Until it is, the default
position applies: under US law the author holds copyright unless it is assigned
in writing, so absent an agreement the studio retains it and the restaurant has
no recorded right to reuse or modify the code.

That is almost certainly not what either side assumes. Pick one and replace this
file.

OPTION A — The restaurant owns it outright
    Normal when a site is billed as work-for-hire and the client expects to take
    it to another developer later. Requires a written assignment; "work made for
    hire" does not attach automatically to commissioned software.

        Copyright (c) 2026 Quik Burrito. All rights reserved.
        Developed by Noir Echelon (https://www.noirechelon.tech/) and assigned
        to Quik Burrito under the parties' written agreement.

OPTION B — The studio owns it and licenses it to the restaurant
    Normal when the studio reuses its own framework across clients. The licence
    should say plainly what the restaurant may do: run the site, have it hosted
    and maintained, and, if agreed, have another developer modify it.

        Copyright (c) 2026 Noir Echelon. All rights reserved.
        Licensed to Quik Burrito for operation of its own website under the
        parties' written agreement.

WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT COVER
    Third-party components keep their own licences regardless of which option is
    chosen — see LICENSING.md. The restaurant's logo, brand and any photographs
    they supply remain theirs and are not transferred by either option.

This is not legal advice. Whichever option is chosen should match what the
signed agreement between the two parties actually says.
